## Hello.xtl -- the facade of the hello extension, the smallest X_eTaL
## native extension: each function proves one thing about the boundary.
## Import: "hx:" u_se< "Hello"
## Run with xetal-x (xetal-x --ext DIR puts this directory on XETAL_PATH).
## Each line below is a native function's signature; the binding macro
## (lib/Ffi.xtlm, docs/ffi-macro.md) writes the facade function from it.

"ffi:" u_se< "Ffi"

## The answer, 42.
"a_nswer : unit -> int"        ffi:b_ind< "hello/answer"
## Left plus right, added natively.
"a_dd : float float -> float"  ffi:b_ind< "hello/add"
## The text in upper case (Unicode).
"s_hout : text -> text"        ffi:b_ind< "hello/shout"
## The sum of every element of a numeric array.
"s_um : float -> float"        ffi:b_ind< "hello/sum"
## A numeric array sent across and back: the same values and shape.
"e_cho : float -> floats"      ffi:b_ind< "hello/echo"
## Always fails: a native error is an X_eTaL error.
"f_ail : unit -> int"          ffi:b_ind< "hello/fail"
## Always panics inside Rust: contained, reported as an X_eTaL error.
"p_anic : unit -> int"         ffi:b_ind< "hello/panic"

## The native package behind these functions (a literal l: name also
## makes this file a library to `xetal type`: ask E7).
l:package := "hello"
